UPDATE products
SET
  story = '<p>It started with a teahouse. We were sitting in Heming Teahouse in People''s Park, drinking gaiwan tea, when someone said: "Close your eyes and just listen." The sound was unlike anything we had recorded before — the clink of porcelain lids, the pour of boiling water, the low hum of retired men arguing about chess, a sparrow that had wandered under the awning. It was not music. It was something better: a city being itself, without trying.</p>

<p>That moment became a project. Over the following weeks, we carried a field recorder into opera houses, night markets, temple courtyards, subway platforms, and living rooms. We captured a flight attendant announcing in Sichuan dialect, a hotpot reaching its rolling boil, a blind date where the man said "up to you" to everything. Forty-five tracks, each under thirty seconds — because a city''s character does not need a full song to reveal itself. It shows up in the gap between two words, in the breath before a laugh, in the sound a teacup makes when it meets the table just right.</p>',

  culture = '<p>Chengdu is a city that sounds different from everywhere else in China. It is not the fastest city, not the loudest, not the most modern. It is the city where people sit down for tea and do not stand up for two hours. Where opera begins with a gong that silences the whole room. Where a dialect softens every consonant until even an argument sounds like affection.</p>

<p>These recordings matter because sound is the first thing a place loses when it develops. Old buildings can be rebuilt and festivals can be revived, but the specific noise of a morning market — the exact pitch of a vendor calling out the price of peppers — exists only in the moment it happens, then disappears forever. Every track in this album is a small act of preservation: a thirty-second window into a Chengdu that is changing faster than anyone expected, captured before it quiets down.</p>

<p>Sichuan opera alone contributes seven tracks, because its opening rhythm is a three-hundred-year-old language that still makes an entire theatre go silent. The dialect tracks matter because Sichuanese is not just Mandarin with an accent — it is a tonal system of its own, and the way a Chengdu grandmother says "eat" carries warmth that no textbook can teach.</p>',

  how_to_use = '<p><strong>After purchase</strong>, your download page appears immediately — no waiting, no shipping. Every track is a 320kbps MP3 file, compatible with any device, phone, or audio software.</p>

<p><strong>For listening</strong>: Put on headphones, close your eyes, and play a track on repeat. Thirty seconds is long enough to transport you somewhere. Try "Tea Awakening" on a stressful morning, or "Jinli Night Scene" when you miss being in a crowd.</p>

<p><strong>For creators</strong>: Use these recordings as ambient beds in videos, podcasts, meditation apps, or sound installations. Layer them under narration, loop them for background atmosphere, or sample them in music. Attribution is appreciated but not required.</p>

<p><strong>For teaching</strong>: If you teach Chinese culture, linguistics, or urban studies, these field recordings give students an authentic sensory entry point — play "Sichuan Opera Opening" before a lecture on regional theatre, or "Hotpot Boiling" when discussing food as social ritual.</p>

<p><strong>File organisation</strong>: Tracks are grouped by category — Opera, Dialect, Teahouse, Daily Life, Cityscape — so you can quickly find the right sound for any moment.</p>'

WHERE slug = 'chengdu-sound-map';
